<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck</h1><p class="st-meta">1226 CE – 1937 CE · capital Lübeck</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Queen of the Hanse, a Baltic port whose law and merchants shaped dozens of towns from Tallinn to Gdańsk and which stayed a free state until 1937.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trave">Trave</a> in 1159 as a German port to the Baltic. After his fall the town came under the emperor, and in 1226 Frederick II granted it the status of a free imperial city. Its merchants soon led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, which held its diets in Lübeck.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of merchant families ruled the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law was adopted by about a hundred Baltic towns. Wealth came from trade in herring, salt, grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, visible in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holstentor">Holstentor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-marys-church-lubeck">St. Mary's Church</a>. Lübeck turned Lutheran in 1530, and under the mayor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurgen-wullenwever">Jürgen Wullenwever</a> it waged the disastrous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1534 to 1536.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanse</a> faded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> declined. It was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1811 to 1813, then joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> as a city-state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> of 1937 merged it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck.jpg" alt="Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_City_of_L%C3%BCbeck.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1226 CE – 1937 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lübeck</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jürgen Wullenwever</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lübeck, Free Imperial City of Lübeck</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck" aria-label="Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 328 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5237 584L5229 694M5237 584L5237 584M5237 584L5054 728M5237 584L5300 622M5237 584L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5237 584h0M5054 728h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lübeck. <a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
